  "prompt_text": "The figure compares how two common Gram-positive bacteria, *Streptococcus pyogenes* and *Staphylococcus aureus*, affect different parts of the human body through inflammation and immune disruption. A central body silhouette is annotated with disease outcomes linked to specific organs or tissues.\n\nOn the left, *S. pyogenes* is associated mainly with throat infection and immune-mediated complications. It promotes pharyngeal infection, can drive T cell–dependent killing of B cells leading to immunosuppression, and repeated infections appear linked to rheumatic heart disease. It is also shown causing systemic inflammation that can trigger toxic shock syndrome and a delayed hypersensitivity reaction leading to scarlet fever.\n\nOn the right, *S. aureus* is connected to a broader range of local and systemic inflammatory conditions. In the upper airway it promotes chronic nasal sinus inflammation, while in the lung its immunomodulatory effects can contribute to pneumonia. It is also linked to toxic shock syndrome, endocarditis, liver bacterial persistence and abscess formation, skin inflammation that worsens atopic dermatitis, food poisoning in the gut, and menstrual toxic shock through TSST-1 activity in the vaginal canal.\n\nOverall, the figure emphasizes organ-specific consequences of bacterial virulence and inflammation across the body.",
